\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The trace integral meets a Gaussian}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Let #{I(t)} be the integral in \ref{fdip-00G2}, and write
#{G(t)=(4\pi t)^{-1/2}}. The series for the exponential
gives #{e>27/10}. Also #{\pi<22/7}. For every #{t\ge 1},
##{\lvert I(t)-G(t)\rvert\le 5\frac{G(t)}{t}.}
The Gaussian moments used below are
##{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\ue^{-t\theta^2}\,\extd\theta=G(t),}
##{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}t\theta^2\ue^{-t\theta^2}\,\extd\theta
=\frac{G(t)}{2},}
##{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}t\theta^4\ue^{-t\theta^2}\,\extd\theta
=\frac{3}{4}\frac{G(t)}{t}.}}

\proof{
\p{The partial sum
#{1+1+1/2+1/6+1/24+1/120=163/60} exceeds #{27/10}, and
the remaining series terms are positive, so #{e>27/10}.
The polynomial identity
#{x^4(1-x)^4=(1+x^2)(x^6-4x^5+5x^4-4x^2+4)-4}
writes the positive integrand
#{x^4(1-x)^4/(1+x^2)} as that polynomial minus
#{4/(1+x^2)}. Its integral from #{0} to #{1} equals
#{1/7-2/3+1-4/3+4-\pi=22/7-\pi}, so #{\pi<22/7}. The
antiderivative of #{1/(1+x^2)} is the arctangent, and
the arctangent of #{1} is #{\frac{\pi}{4}}.}

\p{Let #{S=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\ue^{-u^2}\,\extd u}. The square
#{S^2} is the plane integral of #{\ue^{-(u^2+v^2)}}. Polar
coordinates, with area element #{r\,\extd r\,\extd\varphi}, give
#{\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\infty}\ue^{-r^2}r\,\extd r\,\extd\varphi=\pi},
so #{S=\sqrt{\pi}}. The substitution #{u=\sqrt{t}\,\theta}
gives #{\int \ue^{-t\theta^2}\,\extd\theta=\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{t}}}, and
division by #{2\pi} produces #{G(t)}. For #{t\ge 1} the
#{t}-derivative of the integrand is dominated by the
integrable function #{\theta^2\ue^{-\theta^2}}. Differentiating
under the integral once, and then again, produces the second
and fourth moments. Division by #{2\pi} and multiplication
by #{t} give the displayed moment identities.}

\p{The degree-#{3} Taylor polynomial of the cosine is
#{1-\theta^2/2}, and the Lagrange remainder is
#{\frac{\theta^4\cos\xi}{24}}. Hence
#{\ell(\theta)-\theta^2=-(\theta^4/12)\cos\xi} and
#{\lvert\ell(\theta)-\theta^2\rvert\le\theta^4/12}. On the
interval from #{0} to #{\frac{\pi}{2}} the second derivative of
the sine is #{-\sin}, which is nonpositive, so the graph
lies above its chord. Thus #{\sin\frac{\theta}{2}\ge\frac{\theta}{\pi}}
for #{\theta} between #{0} and #{\pi}, and
#{\ell(\theta)\ge\frac{4\theta^2}{\pi^2}} on the interval from
#{-\pi} to #{\pi}.}

\p{Set #{\delta=t^{-1/4}}. For #{t\ge 1} one has
#{0<\delta\le 1<\pi}. Split #{I-G} into the region
#{\lvert\theta\rvert\le\delta}, the Gaussian tail
#{\lvert\theta\rvert>\delta}, and the region
#{\delta<\lvert\theta\rvert\le\pi} of the periodic
integrand. On the first region
#{\lvert t(\ell(\theta)-\theta^2)\rvert\le 1/12}. For
#{0<x<1} the geometric series gives #{e^x<1/(1-x)}, so
#{\ue^{1/12}<12/11}. Therefore
#{\lvert \ue^{-t\ell}-\ue^{-t\theta^2}\rvert
\le \ue^{-t\theta^2}(t\theta^4/12)\ue^{1/12}}.
Extending the integral and using the fourth-moment
identity bounds this piece by
#{(12/11)(1/12)(3/4)G(t)/t=(3/44)G(t)/t}.}

\p{The Gaussian tail satisfies
#{\int_{\delta}^{\infty}\ue^{-t\theta^2}\,\extd\theta
\le \ue^{-t\delta^2}/(2t\delta)},
because #{\frac{\theta}{\delta}\ge 1} on that ray and the
resulting integral is elementary. Both rays together
contribute at most #{G(t)/t}: with #{s=\sqrt{t}}, the
quotient of this contribution by #{G(t)/t} equals
#{\frac{s^{3/2}\ue^{-s}}{\sqrt{\pi}}}. Because #{\pi>3},
#{\sqrt{\pi}>1}, and #{s^{3/2}\ue^{-s}<1} for #{s\ge 1}.
Indeed #{\log s\le s-1}. On the interval from #{1} to
#{3}, #{\frac{3}{2}(s-1)\le s}, hence
#{\frac{3}{2}\log s\le s}. For #{s>3} the derivative of
#{s-(3/2)\log s} is positive, and the value at #{3} is
positive because #{\log 3<2}, which follows from
#{e^2>7>3}.}

\p{On the outer region the chord bound gives
#{\ue^{-t\ell(\theta)}\le\ue^{-\frac{4t}{\pi^2}\theta^2}}. The same
tail estimate, with this quadratic rate, has quotient
#{\frac{\pi^{3/2}}{4}s^{3/2}\ue^{-\frac{4s}{\pi^2}}} against #{G(t)/t}.
Since #{\pi<22/7}, one has #{\pi^2<484/49} and
#{\frac{4}{\pi^2}>49/121}. Also
#{\frac{\pi^{3/2}}{4}<7/5}, because
#{(22/7)^{3/2}<28/5} follows from
#{22\cdot 3025=66550<67228=7\cdot 9604}, and
#{28/5} divided by #{4} equals #{7/5}. The
maximum of #{u^{3/2}\ue^{-u}} is at most #{1/2}, because
#{e^u/u^{3/2}} has its only positive critical point at
#{u=3/2} and #{\ue^{3/2}\ge 2(3/2)^{3/2}} follows from
#{e^3>27/2}, itself a consequence of #{e>27/10}. With
#{\beta=49/121} one has
#{\beta^{-3/2}=(11/7)^3=1331/343}. Multiplying by
#{1/2} and by #{7/5} gives #{9317/3430}, which is less
than #{3}. Adding the three pieces gives at most
#{(3/44+1+3)G(t)/t=(179/44)G(t)/t}, which is at most
#{5G(t)/t}.}
}
